\details{
Wraps the ESPN core-v2 endpoint
\code{events/{game_id}/competitions/{game_id}/competitors/{team_id}/roster}.
This wrapper reads the teams list for the game and fetches the
roster resource for \strong{both} teams, stacking the \code{entries} arrays into
one long frame -- one row per (team x rostered player). \code{home_away}
identifies which team a row belongs to. Players are returned as ESPN
athlete ids; join to another athlete source for names.

When \code{position_detail = TRUE} (the default) the ESPN position catalog
(\code{\link[=espn_cfb_positions]{espn_cfb_positions()}}) is fetched once and joined onto \code{position_id},
so the output carries the full position name / abbreviation rather than
the bare id (see \emph{Details}).

When \code{position_detail = TRUE} (the default), five columns are appended
by joining the ESPN position catalog (\code{\link[=espn_cfb_positions]{espn_cfb_positions()}}) on
\code{position_id}: \code{position_name}, \code{position_display_name},
\code{position_abbreviation}, \code{position_leaf}, and \code{position_parent_id}.
The catalog is fetched once per call. A roster entry whose
\code{position_id} is missing or unmatched receives \code{NA} for all five, and a
catalog-fetch failure degrades the whole set to \code{NA} rather than
erroring the wrapper. With \code{position_detail = FALSE} the five columns
are omitted and the original roster schema is returned unchanged.
}
